seektest - read-only DEC RA seek sweep, 2.11BSD/PDP-11

Build: make
Install (as root): make install
Default BINDIR is /usr/bin; override with make BINDIR=/some/path install.

Usage: seektest [-n] [-l lowercyl] [-u uppercyl] unit [step [delay_ms]]
  seektest 1           sweep RA unit 1, step 1 cylinder, no delay
  seektest ra1 8       sweep in 8-cylinder steps
  seektest 1 0         alternate the two extremes continuously
  seektest rra1 16 20  16-cylinder steps, 20ms pause after each read
  seektest -n 1        inspect label/device only, no sweep reads
  seektest -l 100 -u 300 ra1 8 100
                      sweep cylinders 100..300, step 8, 100ms delay
  seektest -n -l 100 -u 300 ra1
                      validate limits and show geometry without sweep reads
  seektest -h          help
Stop with Ctrl-C. Raw device permissions normally require root.
Unit means the BSD device number (0..31), not the KDA50 cable port.

Limits are inclusive; lower defaults to 0 and upper to the last usable
logical cylinder. Options precede the unit and may appear in any order.
Values must be unsigned decimal integers within the disk's usable range,
with lower <= upper. Missing values, overflow and repeated limits are
rejected. A single-cylinder interval is permitted. -n validates limits too.

The sweep reads the lower cylinder, upper cylinder, lower+X, upper-X, etc.
After the endpoints cross it restarts. A meeting cylinder is read once.
Step 0 repeats the outer/inner pair indefinitely. Each target performs
lseek followed by one word-aligned 512-byte raw character-device read.
The program opens O_RDONLY and issues only the label-get ioctl; no writes.

DIOCGDINFO supplies sector size, sectors per cylinder and total sectors.
The largest nonempty zero-offset partition supplies a usable range. The
sweep is bounded by both that partition and the label unit size; geometry
can round up beyond the usable partition (as on the RA82). A clipping
notice is printed in that case. Partition c is not assumed to be the
whole disk. -n also prints each partition size and offset.
The last target is the first sector of the last (possibly partial)
logical cylinder, derived from total sectors, so it stays inside the pack.
The RA driver default label has no total-sector field; in that case the
size comes from its largest zero-offset partition (the MSCP drive size),
and a synthetic-geometry notice is printed. An invalid label, missing
zero-offset raw partition or unsupported
32-bit byte-offset range is rejected. No RA60/RA82 geometry is hard-coded.

MSCP addresses logical blocks. Label geometry may be synthetic, and drive
remapping/controller caching can affect the physical movement heard.
Raw reads bypass the UNIX buffer cache; every target requests disk I/O.
Delays use select with long timeval fields (not the inconsistent native
usleep prototype). Resolution is approximately one clock tick; short
requested delays may produce no pause. This generates sustained read load
until interrupted and may compete with normal disk activity.

The source uses K&R function definitions and 32-bit long offset arithmetic.
Build natively on 2.11BSD; macOS lacks this disk-label interface.

Plain make reuses compiler/c0 and compiler/c1 when present, or prepares
private passes from the sibling ksh/native-tools when they are built.
Otherwise it uses ../tools/cpp and ../tools/c1 with the stock compiler.
Explicit PDPFLAGS overrides automatic selection. Selection runs in a
separate shell because the native make shell enables -e, which wrongly
exits on false conditions on this installation.

On mentec the stock passes also have a stack-growth fault. Build with:
  /bin/sh ./build-mentec.sh
This selects the existing sibling ksh/native-tools/c0 and c1, plus the
preprocessor wrapper, through a generated compiler/ directory. Override
PDP_COMPILER_PASSES if those private passes live elsewhere. For a clean
rebuild: make clean; /bin/sh ./build-mentec.sh

Every read checks its sector and byte-offset bounds before seeking.
A hardware error stops the sweep; it is not retried. MSCP status 0353
indicates a drive-detected error. Do not repeat a sweep that faults a
drive; inspect its fault indication and controller diagnostics first.

Host regression checks (simulated devices; no raw disk access):
  python3 tests/test_seektest.py
  python3 tests/test_build.py

Mentec MSCP error reporting:
  Enable immediately as root with the driver's supported sysctl:
    /bin/sysctl -w machdep.mscp.printf=3
  Verify with: /bin/sysctl machdep.mscp.printf
  This enables existing error reports plus MSCP error datagrams. It does
  not require a reboot or any change to kern.securelevel. Install
  rc.mscp-reporting as /etc/rc.mscp-reporting and call it from /etc/rc.local
  to retain the setting across boots. The utility does not send an MSCP
  AVAILABLE/spin-down command; the driver's close routine only drains I/O.
